{
  "requirement": "When implementation shows that agreed requirement text must change, the `habeas-requirements` skill MUST direct agents to stop work that depends on the change and take the changed requirements through review, agreement, and commit before continuing.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligations.** `requirements.cli.requirements_review_skill.text_change` says that, when implementation shows agreed requirement text must change, the `habeas-requirements` skill must direct agents to do two things:\n1. Stop work that depends on the change.\n2. Take the changed requirements through review, agreement and commit before continuing.\n\n**Test.** `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_requirements_skill_stops_dependent_work_when_agreed_text_must_change]` is marked `{test}` for the requirement. It makes two checks.\n\n1. `assert_phrases(\"habeas-requirements\", …)` asserts that the bundled skill text, with whitespace collapsed by `collapse` so the wrapped line matches, contains this sentence: \"If implementation shows that agreed text must change, stop work that depends on it and take the changed requirements through steps 3 to 5 first\". That sentence is in the Workflow section of `assets/skills/habeas-requirements/SKILL.md`, in the paragraph after the numbered steps. It covers the trigger, the \"stop dependent work\" directive, and the routing through steps 3 to 5.\n2. The test asserts that `step(\"habeas-requirements\", n)` starts with \"Review.\" for step 3, \"Present\" for step 4 and \"Commit.\" for step 5. This ties the phrase \"steps 3 to 5\" to review, agreement and commit. The skill's step 3 is the subagent review, step 4 is presenting each group for the user's agreement (with a gate), and step 5 is the commit. A renumbering or reordering of the workflow that broke the reference would fail the test.\n\n**Mapping and gaps.**\n- Every obligation clause has an asserting check: the trigger, stopping dependent work, review, agreement and commit.\n- The requirement has no boundary or failure cases beyond these, and no other modes.\n- The phrase search covers the whole skill text rather than one section. The sentence is specific, though, so it is unlikely to match anywhere other than the intended guidance.\n- A skill that dropped or weakened the directive, for example by omitting \"stop work that depends on it\", or that mapped it to the wrong steps, would fail the test.\n- No unmarked relevant tests or missing scenarios turned up. No `{fixme}` acknowledgments apply.\n\nThe test adequately checks the requirement.",
